WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.700
- [Narrator] For the first time ever,

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.870
college athletes are
able to use their status

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.300
to strike endorsement deals, sponsorships,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.980
and even charge for appearance
fees and commercials,

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:10.060
like this one.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.050
- College athletes can
be in commercials now?

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:13.030
- Dr. Pepper,

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:14.480
still one DJ deserves.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.410
- [Narrator] Last July,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.420
a new NCAA policy allowed college athletes

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.810
to start earning money on endorsements.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:22.760
After pressure from state lawmakers

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.230
and a related Supreme Court
ruling that warned the NCAA

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.450
that broadly limiting athlete compensation

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.434
would violate antitrust laws.

00:00:30.434 --> 00:00:34.170
- There's this batch of 500,000
student athletes every year

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.400
that can now monetize their
name, image and likeness.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.260
And so, I think overall,

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.950
you do have a market that will grow

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.480
into a multi-billion dollar market.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.160
- [Narrator] Opendorse
says brands are on track

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.010
to pay over $579 million
to student athletes

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.930
in the first 12 months since
the Supreme Court ruling.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.210
- I've been able to do
some really cool things

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:58.330
in the past few months
since the rule change.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:01.910
I've gotten to work with
some amazing brands.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:05.230
Yasso Greek Yogurt bars
are one of my favorite

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.500
mindful indulgences.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.900
- [Narrator] So how exactly
are these new rules playing out

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.150
in this new era for college sports,

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.693
and what are student
athletes doing to cash in?

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.410
College athletes have long been classified

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.630
as amateurs by the NCAA,

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.540
even as they helped generate billions

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.250
for their colleges and universities.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.160
The rule change allowing
college athletes to profit

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.640
off of their name, image and likeness,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:30.910
or NIL,

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.690
came after years of mounting
legal and political pressure.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.130
- There was a handful of
states led by California

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.410
that pushed forward
legislation at the state level

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:43.170
that would allow college athletes
to sign endorsement deals.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.180
And in order to get ahead
of college athletes,

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.013
in one state,

00:01:47.013 --> 00:01:49.060
being able to profit off
their name, image and likeness

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:49.910
and not others,

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.170
the NCAA changed its rules.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.310
- [Narrator] So far, 28 states
have adopted legislation

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.957
that gives student athletes
more control over their NIL.

00:01:58.957 --> 00:02:01.960
- It basically says that
if your state has a law

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.700
that lets you profit from
their name image, and likeness,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:05.540
go ahead.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:06.373
If your state doesn't,

00:02:06.373 --> 00:02:08.170
you can still go ahead and do it,

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:11.030
but under the limitations
that NCAA has quiet set.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:11.940
- [Narrator] The new policy

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.740
impacts more than 480,000 athletes

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.060
across the NCAA's three divisions.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.170
Opendorse, a sports technology company

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.860
that helps navigate athletes
through this new landscape

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.150
says that some of the
highest profile athletes

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.610
have made over $100,000
in the first six months

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.510
since the new rules took effect.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.360
For instance, Paige Bueckers,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.630
a basketball star at the
University Of Connecticut,

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.140
became the first college player

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.860
to sign an endorsement deal with Gatorade.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.500
- [Paige] I won national
Gatorade Player of The Year

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.333
in high school

00:02:40.333 --> 00:02:42.340
and that's when our
relationship kind of started.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:43.810
- [Narrator] The financial
details of the deal

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:44.960
weren't disclosed,

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.700
but Gatorade said it is
a multi-year partnership.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.270
Jim Cavale is the CEO of INFLCR,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.260
a mobile app that is helping
over 100,000 athletes

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.050
build their brand on social media

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:57.373
and navigate the new NIL landscape.

00:02:57.373 --> 00:02:59.460
- Part of gaming it is you gotta do social

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.580
in your brand like an influencer does.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:02.970
Most athletes think

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.520
it's just about playing well on the court,

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:05.630
but it's both.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:06.500
When you do both,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.362
that's when you can explode this thing.

00:03:08.362 --> 00:03:10.310
- [Narrator] INFLCR says
the average deal size

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.050
for student athletes on its
platform is around $1,100.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.570
- You see a handful of athletes

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:19.780
actually make six and
seven figures pretty fast,

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.910
but then you see thousands of athletes

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:25.560
do $20 t-shirt deals with Barstool.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.250
And so it's a really wide spectrum.

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:28.500
- [Narrator] According to Opendorse,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.890
national brands account for 57.75%

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.440
of student athlete NIL compensation,

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.350
while local brands make up the rest.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:37.620
- So you have, you know,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.380
maybe a national food chain
like Zaxby's or Chick-fil-A,

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.030
but then it's the local franchises

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.430
that are partnering with
players of a certain team,

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.094
or lots of car dealerships.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:49.380
- Hello, Myles Brennan.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.984
I'm LSU's quarterback.

00:03:50.984 --> 00:03:52.700
(car engine revving)

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.870
- [Narrator] Not all brand deals
result in monetary payouts.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:57.740
Athletes can partner with a sports brand

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.390
in exchange for free gear

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:02.000
or get sponsored by a
restaurant for free meals.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.390
- And there are other athletes

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.770
that have been able to
have smaller deals as well,

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:07.940
where it's maybe, you know,

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.140
the Arkansas offensive lineman,

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.700
they get a gift card to a
restaurant and they post about it,

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.830
and they raved about the meal online.

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:14.820
- [Narrator] A major asset

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.680
many student athletes bring to the table

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.290
is their social media following,

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.270
particularly on Instagram.

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.230
Factors like posting frequently

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:23.870
and having a high engagement rate

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.200
can drive up an athlete's value.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.800
- All those things determine a valuation

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.730
that could be anywhere
from 50 cents per follower

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:35.840
to more than a dollar per follower.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:36.880
- [Narrator] Trinity Thomas,

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.840
a gymnast at the University Of Florida

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.810
has over 54,000 followers on Instagram.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.880
By posting more than
once or twice per month,

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:46.790
she could potentially
see a $40,000 windfall.

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:48.930
- I think the most exciting part is like,

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.090
kinda not feeling like gated in.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.280
Like, you can kinda just do what you love

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.510
and try new things and kinda
put yourself out there.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.840
- [Narrator] Another way college athletes

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.360
profit from their skills
is by giving lessons.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.480
While they weren't banned
from doing this before,

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.960
they couldn't mention their
status as a collegiate athlete

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.470
in promoting their services.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.890
- And so with the new NIL opportunities,

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:12.020
I hope to be able to like,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.760
kinda organize a clinic
and also do some camps,

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.650
maybe even one of my own.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:18.730
- [Narrator] many student athletes

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:20.600
have sizable social media followings,

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.930
but aren't headed for
professional careers.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:24.810
This is the case for Leah Clapper,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.120
another gymnast at the
University Of Florida,

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.690
who is trying to maximize
her time as a student athlete

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:31.900
before graduating in 2023.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.820
- A big, huge venture that I started

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.870
was a gymnastics board game company,

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.650
and that would not have
been possible before NIL.

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.270
So I teamed up with one
of my former coaches

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.010
and we created a gymnastics board game

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:50.360
and we got to use my
status as a Florida Gator

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:51.280
to help market it,

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.820
and use my connections and my
social media platform as well,

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:56.170
which was a huge help.

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:58.960
- [Narrator] The rapidly
evolving landscape of NIL rules

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.660
carries significant implications

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.100
for the future of collegiate
athletic recruitment.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.130
- There's broad fear

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.700
that this is going to lead
to a pay for play model

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.800
where the richest schools
get the best athletes,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:09.720
and that there's going to be

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.660
lots of bad recruiting inducements.

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.690
And this is something that the NCAA

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.610
has been battling in college sports

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:17.760
for pretty much as long as it's existed.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.990
- [Narrator] Loose
guidelines set by the NCAA

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:22.110
have left a lot up to interpretation

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.420
as colleges navigate various
compliance challenges.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:28.360
- The one broad thing
that all these rules say

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.860
is that the schools can't
actively pay a player,

00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:33.250
but there's nothing saying that,

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:35.730
oh, a coach can't have a
conversation with boosters

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.350
when he's at a lunch with donors and say,

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:38.430
yeah, it'd be really great

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.480
if we got some great NIL deals
lined up for my athletes,

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:44.750
and it's unclear if that's
against the rules right now.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.130
- [Narrator] It's uncertain when

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.140
or if the federal government will step in

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:49.840
to regulate NIL rules.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.400
But for now, student
athletes are taking advantage

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.290
of a new profitable era in college sports.

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:59.470
- Athletes can get a headstart
on financial independence.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:04.283
I honestly think that it's
changed the course of my career.

00:07:07.648 --> 00:07:10.231
(gentle music)

